<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Gradle dependency verification — the Podfile-checksum analogue for Android.

  WHAT THIS IS. iOS pins LibSignalClient by git commit PLUS a fail-closed
  sha256 over the prebuilt FFI archive (app/ios/Podfile). Android's equivalent
  mechanism is this file: Gradle refuses to use an artifact whose bytes do not
  hash to the value recorded here. The pin covers all three files Gradle
  fetches for each module — the AAR/JAR, the POM, and the Gradle module
  metadata — because the .module file is what actually drives variant
  selection, and a POM-only pin would leave the file that chooses the bytes
  unpinned.

  WHY THE SCOPE IS ORG.SIGNAL PLUS THE FCM FAMILY, AND NOTHING ELSE. The
  Podfile pins ONE dependency's checksum, not the whole CocoaPods graph, and
  this is that same claim, held at two dependencies now instead of one:
  libsignal (a substitution would be a cryptographic compromise) and
  the Firebase/Play-services family behind
  firebase-messaging (`com.google.firebase`, `com.google.android.gms`,
  `com.google.android.datatransport`, plus the `com.google.gms`
  google-services buildscript plugin), because those are the bytes that
  decide when and how this phone can be WOKEN, and a substituted push stack
  is a security compromise, not a build break. Pinning the entire Android
  graph would add several hundred hashes that rot on every React Native
  bump, and a pin file people regenerate on every red build is a pin file
  that verifies nothing. So everything outside those groups is TRUSTED by
  the regex below, and the excluded groups must match a recorded sha256 or
  the build fails. NOTE: the FCM dependencies are themselves CONDITIONAL on
  app/google-services.json existing (app/build.gradle's FCM header); their
  pins sit here unconditionally, waiting, because an unresolved pin costs
  nothing and a resolution without a pin is what this file forbids.

  PROVENANCE OF THE HASHES. org.signal: downloaded 2026-08-18 from
  https://build-artifacts.signal.org/libraries/maven and hashed locally with
  `shasum -a 256`; each value was then cross-checked against the sha256 the
  publisher records inside its own Gradle module metadata (the .module files
  list sha256/sha512/sha1/md5 per file, and all four agree with the bytes on
  disk). Two independent statements of the same digest, not one. The
  com.google.* family: recorded 2026-08-24 by Gradle's own
  write-verification-metadata sha256 task (the "Generated by Gradle"
  origin entries) over artifacts resolved from dl.google.com — Google's first-party
  repository, over TLS — during the both-states build proof; the value
  of these pins is FREEZING that first observation, so any later substitution
  (a mirror, a poisoned cache, a republished version) fails loudly against
  the bytes this rig verified and built with on that date.

  WHICH PINS ARE LOAD-BEARING, measured rather than assumed (2026-08-18).
  Corrupting one digit of the .module hash, or of the AAR hash, and resolving
  with a cleared cache FAILS the build — "Dependency verification failed …
  This can indicate that a dependency has been compromised". Corrupting the
  .pom hash alone does NOT: these modules publish Gradle module metadata, so
  the .pom is fetched but never consulted for resolution, and Gradle verifies
  what it uses. The .pom entries stay because they cost nothing and would
  become load-bearing the day a consumer resolves through POM metadata — but
  they are recorded here as belt-and-braces, not as the control, because a
  pin file that oversells which of its lines actually bite is the same defect
  as an audit that cannot fail.

  TO ADD A VERSION: fetch the artifacts, hash them, and add a <component>
  block — or, for the FCM family, place a google-services.json and rerun
  ./gradlew with the write-verification-metadata sha256 flag (two leading
  hyphens, which an XML comment cannot spell) and :app:assembleDebug, then
  restore this header (the write task strips XML comments below the root).
  Never widen the trust rule to cover org.signal or the FCM groups.
-->
<verification-metadata xmlns="https://schema.gradle.org/dependency-verification" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="https://schema.gradle.org/dependency-verification https://schema.gradle.org/dependency-verification/dependency-verification-1.3.xsd">
   <configuration>
      <verify-metadata>true</verify-metadata>
      <!-- Neither Signal nor Google publishes PGP signatures for these
           artifacts; the sha256 pins below are the whole control, which is
           why they cover the metadata files too. -->
      <verify-signatures>false</verify-signatures>
      <trusted-artifacts>
         <trust group="^(?!org[.]signal$|com[.]google[.]firebase$|com[.]google[.]android[.]gms$|com[.]google[.]android[.]datatransport$|com[.]google[.]gms$).*$" regex="true" reason="Everything except org.signal and the four FCM groups is trusted: this file is a scoped pin over the two dependencies whose substitution would be a security compromise rather than a build break — libsignal (the crypto), and the Firebase/Play-services family that decides when this phone wakes. Not a whole-graph lockdown."/>
      </trusted-artifacts>
   </configuration>
